Here’s how stock actually moves through the system, from receiving to dispatch.

Two layers: the drug vs. the batch. The key idea is that a product is stored in two levels. A product master is the drug’s identity — its name, molecule, HSN code, GST rate, category (drug, fluid, consumable, premed), and its expected dosing unit. Underneath each master sit batch lots — the actual physical stock you received, each with its own brand, manufacturer, strength (active content per vial/amp/bag), batch number, expiry date, and quantity. So “Vitamin C” is one master, but you might hold two lots of it: batch VC-B7 expiring soon and VC-B9 expiring later, possibly even different brands or strengths. This split is what lets the same drug carry multiple expiries and batches at once, which is how a real pharmacy shelf works.

Receiving stock just means adding a lot under a master — you enter the batch no., expiry, content per unit, and quantity, and it lands as on-hand units. Nothing is “available” in the abstract; availability is always computed from these concrete lots.

How availability is worked out. When you ask how many of a drip you can prepare, the engine looks at every ingredient, pulls only its in-date lots (expired batches are silently excluded — never counted, never dispensed), and subtracts any units already reserved by confirmed orders. Then it divides the usable stock by each ingredient’s dose and takes the lowest — the limiting ingredient is your bottleneck. It reports two numbers: the theoretical pooled count (if every milligram were usable) and the realistic whole-vial count (since single-use vials are indivisible, so a 400 mg dose still consumes a whole 500 mg vial). The gap between them is your wastage.

How stock gets deducted. An order runs through three states. On confirm, the system soft-reserves the exact units it will need — those units still physically exist but are no longer counted as available, so two orders can’t promise the same vial. On dispatch, it actually consumes stock, drawing whole units FEFO (first-expiry-first-out), so the batch closest to expiry drains first. It decrements the lots, releases the reservation, and writes an immutable consumption ledger recording which batch fed which order — that’s your recall and traceability trail. It also deducts the session kit (cannula, IV set) per drip prepared. Cancel simply releases the reservation back to available.

Staying ahead of problems. The Alerts view continuously flags batches expiring within 90 days, expired stock still sitting on the shelf, and any master at or below its reorder level.